# Reference verification, batch 5: s028-s035 (Nov 2026)
upd("n494", append="[Checked: Aug. *De Trin.* VIII 1.1 (prooemium; augustinus.it): ‘nec tamen tres dii aut tres boni aut tres omnipotentes, sed unus Deus, bonus, omnipotens, ipsa Trinitas’. So Gorris’s book VIII is right. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n503", append="[Checked: Wodeham’s Oxford and Paris lectures on *Sent.* I d. 33 are not available online in searchable form. Still open.]")
upd("n504", append="[Checked in Badius 1520 via SCTA: *Summa* a. 54 is on the mode of emanation (q. 3: whether anything emanates from the unemanating person; q. 5: whether the two emanating persons proceed from him equally and immediately). The formula *essentia generat essentiam* is discussed at a. 54 q. 3, and a. 58 q. 2 asks whether *generare* is the same as *dicere*. Gorris’s ‘in summa’ is plausible, but the precise article for the *quidam doctor* is not settled. Still open.]")
upd("n517", append="[Checked: Aug. *De Trin.* VII 6.11 (augustinus.it): ‘tres autem personas ex eadem essentia non dicimus, quasi aliud ibi sit quod essentia est, aliud quod persona’. Verified for VII 6.11; Gorris’s ‘V c. 33’ is wrong.]", status="verified")
upd("n520", append="[Checked: the doctrine that the divine essence is begotten *subiective* is Henry’s. *Summa* a. 59 q. 2 (Badius 1520 via SCTA) has ‘non enim generatur filius obiectiue nisi quia de diuina essentia generatur subiectiue’, prepared at a. 59 q. 1 and applied at q. 3. Godfrey’s *Quodl.* I–VII (SCTA), VIII (Hoffmans) and XI–XIV (Philosophes Belges) gave no parallel for *generari subiective*. So the Vatican’s Henry identification holds, and Gorris’s ‘Godofredus’ is unsupported (though IX–X could not be searched). The locus is corrected to Henry, *Summa* a. 59 qq. 1–3.]", status="verified")
upd("n528", append="[Checked: the Vatican paragraph numbers could not be consulted. The Wadding/Vivès text was not searched for the exact wording. Still open.]")
upd("n530", append="[Checked: the phrase *prima regula contingentium* was not found anywhere in the Wadding/Vivès *Ordinatio* (SCTA). Still open.]")
upd("n534", append="[Checked: Scotus’s first argument for the question in the Wadding/Vivès text of *Ord.* I d. 6 q. un. (SCTA) is from Richard of St Victor, *De Trin.* VI c. 17 (‘ingenitum velle habere de se sibi conformem…’), not from Hilary. Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 6 c. 1 (Quaracchi via SCTA) quotes Hilary’s *De synodis* (‘neque consilio neque voluntate Pater genuit Filium, anathematizat’) on the other side. No Hilary text with *caritate et natura* was found. Still open. Gorris’s authority may be a garbled Richard.]")
upd("n535", append="[Checked: Aug. *De Trin.* XV 20.38 (augustinus.it): ‘Quocirca ridenda est dialectica Eunomii … Filium Dei esse natura’. Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 6 c. 1 (Quaracchi via SCTA) quotes the same passage as ‘in libro XV De Trinitate’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n537", append="[Checked: Rodington’s *Sentences* commentary is unedited, and no text was found online. Still open.]")
upd("n538", append="[Checked: Thomas, *ST* I q. 34 a. 1 co. and ad 3 (Corpus Thomisticum): ‘Anselmus vero improprie accepit dicere pro intelligere. Quae tamen differunt’. Verified for Thomas. Henry’s *Summa* a. 58 q. 2 (‘generare … idem quod dicere’, Badius 1520 via SCTA) is the closer Henry parallel.]", status="verified")
upd("n539", append="[Checked: Henry, *Quodl.* II q. 5 (Badius 1518 via SCTA) is on whether Christ’s body remains when the sacramental species cease. It does not fit, and *intentio copulans* does not occur anywhere in Henry’s Quodlibeta. The reference looks wrong.]", status="uncertain")
upd("n541", append="[Checked: no external source is involved; this is Scotus’s own doctrine and was not checked further. Still open.]")
upd("n542", append="[Checked: Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 6 c. 1 (Quaracchi via SCTA): ‘De hoc Orosius ad Augustinum ita ait: … praeire voluntas sapientiam non potest’. Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 6 q. un. (Wadding/Vivès via SCTA): ‘est Augustini ad Orosium: Preire scientiam voluntas non potest’. Gorris’s *scientiam* matches Scotus’s wording rather than Lombard’s *sapientiam*. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n546", append="[Checked: Aug. *De Trin.* VII 2.3 (augustinus.it): ‘Verbum non ad se dicitur, sed tantum relative ad eum cuius Verbum est’. This is the closest Augustinian wording. The exact dictum was not found. Still open.]")
upd("n547", append="[Checked: Godfrey, *Quodl.* V q. 4 (SCTA): ‘Utrum verbum in divinis sit solum personale soli Filio conveniens vel sit aliquod verbum essentiale in divinis’. This fits the context (*dicere*/*verbum* in d. 6). Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n555", append="[Checked: in *De Trin.* V, VII and XV (augustinus.it) the dictum was not found verbatim. The nearest is VII 6.11, ‘ut substantia Patris ipse Pater est, non quo Pater est, sed quo est’, which is the contrary formula. Still open.]")
upd("n559", append="[Checked: Thomas, *ST* I q. 41 a. 5 co. (Corpus Thomisticum): ‘quidam dixerunt quod potentia generandi significat relationem … Sed hoc esse non potest … natura divina in patre, est potentia generandi in ipso’. Verified for q. 41 a. 5.]", status="verified")
upd("n560", append="[Checked in Badius 1518 via SCTA: Henry, *Quodl.* III q. 3 asks ‘vtrum ratio generandi in patre sit diuina essentia, vel proprietas eius vt pirnitas, vel vtrumque’, which is exactly the point. III q. 5 is on Christ’s soul. So B’s *q. iij.* is right and A’s *q. 5* wrong. The *Summa* a. 57 text on SCTA has only qq. 1–4, so q. 7 could not be checked. Verified for *Quodl.* III q. 3.]", status="verified")
upd("n562", append="[Checked: Bekker references not re-checked against a Greek or Latin text. Still open.]")
upd("n563", append="[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 7 q. 1 in the Wadding/Vivès text (SCTA) is ‘Utrum potentia generandi in Patre sit aliquid absolutum vel proprietas Patris’. Verified as the locus.]", status="verified")
upd("n564", append="[Checked: the Wadding/Vivès text (SCTA) also has only one question in d. 7, so the folding into q. 1 is not peculiar to Gorris. Still open as to the precise passage.]")
upd("n565", append="[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 7 q. 1 (Wadding/Vivès via SCTA) argues from univocal generation (‘impossibile est agens communicare terminum formalem productionis, nisi agat forma aeque perfecta si agit univoce’). The objection that divine generation is *aequivoca* is answered there. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n573", text="The equality of the Holy Spirit *propter summam simplicitatem* is in Augustine, *De Trin.* VI 5.7 (augustinus.it): ‘aequalis est igitur etiam Spiritus Sanctus, et si aequalis in omnibus aequalis, propter summam simplicitatem quae in illa substantia est’. B, like A, has *v.* (B checked), so book V is wrong in both printings. The earlier ‘VI 7.9’ has been corrected.", status="verified")
upd("n574", append="[Checked: *resolubile* and *simpliciter simplex* were not found in the Wadding/Vivès d. 8 p. 1 q. 1. The Vatican numbers were not checked. Still open.]")
upd("n583", append="[Checked: Aug. *De Trin.* VI 6.8 (augustinus.it): ‘Nihil enim simplex mutabile est; omnis autem creatura mutabilis’. This is the dictum, so the paraphrase *nulla est simplex* gives its sense. Gorris’s *c. 5* is off by one chapter. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n585", append="[Checked: *SCG* II c. 49 (Corpus Thomisticum) is ‘Quod substantia intellectualis non sit corpus’. c. 52 is ‘Quod in substantiis intellectualibus creatis differt esse et quod est’, and c. 53 is ‘… est actus et potentia’. Verified for cc. 52–53; Gorris’s c. 49 is wrong.]", status="verified")
upd("n587", append="[Checked: *Ord.* I d. 8 p. 1 q. 2 (Wadding/Vivès via SCTA): ‘nulla creatura est perfecte simplex, quin aliquo modo sit composita vel componibilis’. Verified.]", status="verified")
upd("n590", append="[Checked: Scotus himself cites ‘Avicenna 2. Metaph. C. 1. “Ens in subjecto et ens non in subjecto, non habent medium”’ in *Ord.* I d. 8 p. 1 q. 3 (Wadding/Vivès via SCTA). Verified as Scotus’s citation; the Avicenna Latin was not consulted.]", status="verified")
